# Stealth Rod Co — Dev Workspace

## What this project is
This is a **build workspace**, not the production site. The production site is built in **Webflow**. This repo exists to:
- Write and test custom JS and SCSS with proper tooling (modules, hot reload, etc.)
- Use Astro pages as a dummy markup test harness
- Output a compiled `bundle.js` and `bundle.css` that get manually pasted into Webflow when dev is complete
- During development, Netlify hosts the built bundle so Webflow can pull the script remotely via a `<script src="...">` tag

## Project structure
```
src/
  pages/
    index.astro        # Test harness — dummy markup mirroring Webflow DOM
  scripts/
    src_init.js        # JS bundle entry — DOMContentLoaded bootstrap (paste target via bundle.js)
    src_app.js         # SRC_App class — GSAP context, wires interaction modules
    src_shopify_buybtn.js
    interaction_*.js   # Feature modules (nav, smooth scroll, update loop, etc.)
  styles/
    main.scss          # SCSS entry point (what gets pasted into Webflow)
    _variables.scss    # SCSS variables
build.js               # esbuild + sass entry; supports --watch flag, mirrors output to comet-local-overrides/
comet-local-overrides/ # Comet DevTools Local Overrides folder (gitignored)
  stealthrodco-dev.netlify.app/
    bundle.css         # written by build.js (NOT a symlink — Dropbox doesn't preserve them reliably)
    bundle.js          # written by build.js
netlify.toml           # Build config for Netlify
```

## Build commands
- `npm run dev` — Watch mode: rebuilds `public/bundle.js` and `bundle.css` on save (esbuild watch for JS, chokidar+sass for SCSS). Run this in a Terminal tab during active dev.
- `npm run dev:astro` — Astro dev server (legacy, kept as fallback — rarely used)
- `npm run build:webflow` — One-shot build, outputs `public/bundle.js` + `public/bundle.css` (no Astro)
- `npm run build` — Runs esbuild then Astro build (used by Netlify)

## Netlify deploy
- Git push to main triggers a Netlify deploy
- Built bundle is served at: `https://stealthrodco-dev.netlify.app/bundle.js` and `/bundle.css`
- These URLs are stable across deploys (no hash in filename)
- `public/bundle.js` and `public/bundle.css` are gitignored — they are always built fresh
- During dev, Webflow custom code embeds these Netlify URLs via `<link>` and `<script>` tags
- At launch, those tags get removed and bundle contents are pasted directly into Webflow custom code (see "Launch workflow" below)

## Local dev workflow (Comet local overrides)
Iterate on bundle changes without redeploying to Netlify by intercepting the Netlify URL with a local file via Comet DevTools.

Setup (one-time):
1. In Comet DevTools → Sources → Overrides, click "Select folder for overrides" and pick `comet-local-overrides/` from the project root
2. `build.js` writes bundle output to BOTH `public/` and `comet-local-overrides/stealthrodco-dev.netlify.app/` — no manual file management needed
3. Confirm "Enable Local Overrides" toggle is on

Dev loop:
1. `npm run dev` — leave running in a Terminal tab
2. Edit `.scss` or `.js` → save → Terminal logs `✓ bundle.{css,js} rebuilt`
3. Hard reload Comet (Cmd+Shift+R) on the Webflow page
4. Purple dots in Network tab next to `bundle.css` / `bundle.js` confirm overrides are active

Notes:
- The Webflow Designer canvas does NOT execute custom code — test against the published `*.webflow.io` URL
- Hard reload is required: Comet caches the override response between regular reloads
- Symlinks were tried initially but Dropbox sync resolves them into static file copies, breaking the dev loop. `build.js` does the mirror copy instead — Dropbox-safe
- If overrides stop working after a Comet restart, re-grant the folder permission in DevTools

## Launch workflow (handing off to client)
At project completion the Netlify connection is dropped and bundle contents are pasted directly into Webflow custom code, so the client's site has zero external dependencies.

Steps:
1. `npm run build:webflow` to produce final minified bundles
2. Copy contents of `public/bundle.css` → Webflow → Site Settings → Custom Code → Head Code, paste inside `<style>...</style>`
3. Copy contents of `public/bundle.js` → Webflow → Site Settings → Custom Code → Footer Code, paste inside `<script>...</script>`
4. Remove the existing dev embed lines from custom code:
   - `<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://stealthrodco-dev.netlify.app/bundle.css" />`
   - `<script src="https://stealthrodco-dev.netlify.app/bundle.js" defer></script>`
5. Republish the Webflow site

Capacity:
- Webflow custom code limit: 50,000 chars per field (head, footer, page-level)
- Reference bundle sizes: `bundle.css` ≈ 3.3KB, `bundle.js` ≈ 7.1KB — well under the limit
- Fallbacks if `bundle.js` ever outgrows 50K: host via jsDelivr (public GitHub repo) or keep the Netlify deploy active as a CDN

Future edits after launch:
- Edit locally → `npm run build:webflow` → re-paste both bundles into Webflow → republish
- For active iteration sessions, revive the local-overrides workflow against the dev Netlify URL instead of paste-cycling

## JS conventions
- `src/scripts/src_init.js` is the **only** esbuild entry (`build.js` `entryPoints`) — keep it thin: listeners, one-off setup, then construct `SRC_App` from `src_app.js`
- App behavior and subsystems live in `src_app.js` and `interaction_*.js` / `src_*.js` co-located under `src/scripts/`
- Use ES modules (`import`/`export`) — esbuild bundles everything
- No jQuery — vanilla JS only
- Wrap init logic in `DOMContentLoaded` in `src_init.js` to account for Webflow's load timing
- Astro test pages that need the app should import `../scripts/src_init.js` (same graph the bundle uses)

## SCSS conventions
- `src/styles/main.scss` is the entry point — use `@use` to pull in partials
- Partials live in `src/styles/` and are prefixed with `_`
- Use BEM naming: `.block__element--modifier`
- Use CSS custom properties for values that need to be overridable from Webflow (e.g. `var(--nav-btn-line-color, #222)`)
- Avoid global resets or base styles — Webflow has its own, only write additive styles

## Webflow context
- The production site is in Webflow — this workspace produces assets for it
- Webflow injects jQuery globally — do not conflict with it
- Avoid `w-nav`, `w-nav-menu`, `w-nav-button` classes — these trigger Webflow's built-in navbar JS
- Custom components use a plain `div` root wrapper (not a Webflow Navbar component) to avoid class conflict restrictions
- The root element of a Webflow component cannot be freely classed — put a child `div` inside and style that instead

## Accessibility conventions
- SVG icons used as decorative elements: `aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"`
- SVG icons that are the only label for a button: use `aria-label` on the `<button>`, not on the SVG
- Headings that mix HTML text and SVG text art: use `aria-label` on the heading element with the full readable statement
- Toggle buttons (e.g. menu open/close): use `aria-expanded` toggled between `"true"` / `"false"` via JS
- `transform-box: fill-box` required on SVG elements using CSS `transform-origin: center center`

## Nav component
- Custom-built (not Webflow Navbar component)
- Menu button is a `<button>` with `aria-label`, `aria-expanded`, and `aria-controls`
- Open state is driven by a custom class toggled via JS (not `w--open`)
- SVG menu icon uses two `<path>` elements animated with CSS `translate`, `rotate`, and `transform-box: fill-box`
- CSS custom property `--nav-btn-line-color` controls stroke color with fallback `#222`
